## TL;DR checklist

| Goal | Layer type | Key paint property | Expression |
|------|------------|--------------------|------------|
| **Hypsometric tint** | `color-relief` | `color-relief-color` | `["interpolate", ["linear"], ["elevation"], …]` |
| **Opacity** | same layer | `color-relief-opacity` | number 0‑1 |
| **Resampling (optional)** | same layer | `resampling` | `"linear"` / `"nearest"` |
| **3‑D terrain** | root‑level `terrain` (via `map.setTerrain`) | `source` + `exaggeration` | – |
| **Sky** | root‑level `sky` (via `map.setSky`) | `sky-color`, `horizon-color`, … | – |

That’s all you need to get a client‑side hypsometric colour ramp from a `raster-dem` source in MapLibre GL JS. Happy mapping!

## Short answer
Yes.  
MapLibre can turn a **`raster‑dem`** source into vector contour tiles **on the client** with the tiny helper library **`maplibre‑contour`**.  
The library runs a Web‑Worker that reads the DEM tiles, extracts isolines at the elevations you ask for, and serves them through a custom URL protocol. You then add a normal **`vector`** source + a **`line`** (or `symbol`) layer that points at that protocol URL.

Below is a complete, step‑by‑step recipe that works with the current MapLibre GL JS (≥ v5.6) and the `maplibre‑contour` package (v0.2‑latest). Adjust the URLs, encoding, and styling to fit your own data.

---

## 1. Install / load the library

### npm / Yarn (recommended for a build pipeline)

```bash
npm install maplibre-contour   # or yarn add maplibre-contour
```

### CDN (quick‑start)

```html
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-contour@0.2.2/dist/maplibre-contour.min.js"></script>
```

> The CDN bundle attaches the class `mlcontour.DemSource` to the global `mlcontour` object, exactly as the examples below use.

---

## 2. Prepare your DEM source

Your DEM must be a **`raster-dem`** source that MapLibre can decode.  
Typical encodings are:

| Encoding name | What the tiles contain | Example source |
|---------------|-----------------------|----------------|
| `mapbox`      | 16‑bit signed integer (meters) stored in the red/green channels (Mapbox “terrain‑rgb”) | `https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=YOUR_KEY` |
| `terrarium`  | 16‑bit unsigned integer (meters) stored in RGB, with an offset of 32768 m | `https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp` |
| `raw`        | 16‑bit signed integer in a single band (e.g. GeoTIFF‑like) | custom server |

Make sure the **`encoding`** you pass to `DemSource` matches the tiles you serve, otherwise the elevation values will be wrong.

```js
// Example: a Terrarium‑encoded DEM hosted on a CDN
const demUrl = 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp';
```

---

## 3. Create a `DemSource` and hook it into MapLibre

```js
import mlcontour from 'maplibre-contour';   // ← npm import
// or, if you used the CDN: const mlcontour = window.mlcontour;

const demSource = new mlcontour.DemSource({
  // URL template for the DEM tiles
  url: demUrl,

  // Must match the encoding of the DEM tiles
  encoding: 'terrarium',   // 'mapbox' | 'terrarium' | 'raw'

  // The highest zoom level at which DEM tiles exist.
  // (MapLibre will stop requesting tiles above this.)
  maxzoom: 12,

  // Whether to run the contour extraction in a Web‑Worker.
  // `true` is recommended for any non‑trivial map.
  worker: true
});

// Register the helper with the map instance.
// This adds the custom protocol `mlcontour://` that the library uses.
demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl);
```

> **Why a worker?**  
> Contour extraction is CPU‑intensive (it runs a marching‑squares algorithm on every DEM tile). Off‑loading it to a worker keeps the UI thread smooth.

---

## 4. Add a *virtual* vector source that points at the contour protocol

The library creates a **custom URL protocol** (`mlcontour://`) that returns a vector tile containing the contour lines.  
You tell MapLibre to treat that URL as a normal `vector` source.

```js
// Build the protocol URL with the options you need.
const contourUrl = demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
  // Elevation intervals (in metres) for each zoom level.
  // The object keys are zoom levels; the values are
  // [minorInterval, majorInterval].
  thresholds: {
    // At zoom 11 we want a 200 m minor and 1000 m major grid.
    11: [200, 1000],
    // At zoom 12 we want finer lines.
    12: [100, 500],
    // At higher zooms you can go even finer.
    14: [50, 200]
  },

  // Property names that will appear in each contour feature.
  // `elevationKey` is the numeric height (in metres).
  // `levelKey` is 0 for minor, 1 for major (useful for styling).
  elevationKey: 'ele',
  levelKey: 'level',

  // Optional: give the source a stable name that you can refer to
  // from other parts of the style (e.g. for a label layer).
  contourLayer: 'contours'   // this becomes the source‑layer name
});

// Add the vector source to the map.
map.addSource('contours', {
  type: 'vector',
  // The protocol URL is a *single* tile URL; MapLibre will request
  // it for every tile coordinate (z/x/y) it needs.
  tiles: [contourUrl],
  // The source inherits the maxzoom of the DEM (or you can raise it).
  maxzoom: 15
});
```

> **What the protocol does**  
> When MapLibre asks for tile `z/x/y`, the library fetches the underlying DEM tile(s) that intersect that area, runs the marching‑squares algorithm at the requested thresholds, and returns a tiny vector tile (MVT) that contains a single layer (`contours`) with line features.

---

## 5. Add a line layer (and optionally a label layer)

```js
// 1️⃣ Contour lines
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-lines',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',   // matches the `contourLayer` name above
  paint: {
    // Slightly transparent black for minor lines, darker for majors.
    'line-color': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],   // 0 = minor, 1 = major
      1, 'rgba(0,0,0,0.8)',   // major
      'rgba(0,0,0,0.4)'       // minor (default)
    ],
    // Thicker strokes for major contours.
    'line-width': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],
      1, 1.5,   // major
      0.8      // minor (default)
    ],
    // Optional: a subtle glow to make them visible over imagery.
    'line-blur': 0.5
  }
});

// 2️⃣ Optional: contour labels (show the elevation value)
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-labels',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  layout: {
    // Show the elevation value as text.
    'text-field': ['to-string', ['get', 'ele']],
    // Only label major contours (level === 1) to avoid clutter.
    'text-ignore-placement': false,
    'text-allow-overlap': false,
    'text-size': 12,
    'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular', 'Arial Unicode MS Regular']
  },
  paint: {
    'text-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.7)',
    'text-halo-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.9)',
    'text-halo-width': 1
  },
  filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1]   // only major contours get labels
});
```

That’s it – the map now draws **live contour lines** directly from the DEM tiles, without any pre‑generated vector tileset.

---

## 6. Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>MapLibre – Runtime Contours</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.6.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <style>#map {position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.6.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-contour@0.2.2/dist/maplibre-contour.min.js"></script>
<script>
  // 1️⃣ Initialise the map
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // Base imagery (any raster source you like)
        'sat': {
          type: 'raster',
          tiles: ['https://tiles.stadiamaps.com/tiles/alidade_smooth/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
          tileSize: 256
        }
      },
      layers: [
        { id: 'sat', type: 'raster', source: 'sat' }
      ]
    },
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 45,
    bearing: -17
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 2️⃣ Create the DEM source wrapper
    const dem = new mlcontour.DemSource({
      url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
      encoding: 'terrarium',
      maxzoom: 12,
      worker: true
    });
    dem.setupMaplibre(maplibregl);

    // 3️⃣ Add the virtual vector source
    const contourUrl = dem.contourProtocolUrl({
      thresholds: {11: [200, 1000], 12: [100, 500], 14: [50, 200]},
      elevationKey: 'ele',
      levelKey: 'level',
      contourLayer: 'contours'
    });

    map.addSource('contours', {
      type: 'vector',
      tiles: [contourUrl],
      maxzoom: 15
    });

    // 4️⃣ Add the line layer
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'contour-lines',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'contours',
      'source-layer': 'contours',
      paint: {
        'line-color': [
          'match',
          ['get', 'level'],
          1, 'rgba(0,0,0,0.85)',
          'rgba(0,0,0,0.45)'
        ],
        'line-width': [
          'match',
          ['get', 'level'],
          1, 1.4,
          0.9
        ]
      }
    });

    // 5️⃣ (Optional) Labels for major contours
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'contour-labels',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'contours',
      'source-layer': 'contours',
      filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1],
      layout: {
        'text-field': ['to-string', ['get', 'ele']],
        'text-size': 11,
        'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular', 'Arial Unicode MS Regular'],
        'text-offset': [0, 0.5],
        'text-anchor': 'top'
      },
      paint: {
        'text-color': '#222',
        'text-halo-color': '#fff',
        'text-halo-width': 1
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the page in a browser, pan/zoom, and you’ll see contour lines appear instantly as the DEM tiles are fetched and processed.

---

## 7. Things to keep in mind

| Issue | Why it happens | How to fix / avoid |
|-------|----------------|--------------------|
| **No contours appear** | • The DEM URL is wrong or CORS‑blocked.<br>• `encoding` mismatched (e.g., you told the library “terrarium” but the tiles are Mapbox terrain‑rgb). | Verify the DEM tiles load directly in the browser (e.g., open `https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/12/656/1583.webp`).<br>Check the `encoding` option. |
| **Contours look jagged / missing at high zoom** | The DEM source’s `maxzoom` is lower than the map’s zoom, so no DEM tiles exist for that level. | Either increase the DEM’s maxzoom (serve higher‑resolution tiles) or set `maxzoom` on the vector source higher **and** let the library upsample (it will, but quality drops). |
| **Performance stalls while panning** | Running contour extraction on the main thread (worker = false) or using a very dense threshold set. | Keep `worker: true`. Use coarser `thresholds` at low zooms; you can supply

Below is a **step‑by‑step recipe** that turns the blank “page‑background” you see above the horizon into a real sky with a gentle haze (fog) that blends into the terrain.  
All of the pieces are **native MapLibre GL JS** – no extra plugins are required.

---

## 1. Prerequisites

| What you need | Why it matters |
|---------------|----------------|
| **MapLibre GL JS ≥ 5.5** (the version that introduced the `sky` object) | Earlier versions simply ignore `map.setSky()` |
| A **`raster-dem`** source that you already use for `map.setTerrain()` | The sky (and fog) are only drawn when 3‑D terrain is active |
| The map’s **camera pitched** (you already have `pitch: 70`) | The sky is rendered only when the view is tilted away from nadir |

If any of those are missing, upgrade or add them first.

---

## 2. Add the terrain source (you probably already have this)

```js
// Example – you can keep your own source name / URL
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json', // <-- your DEM tileset
  tileSize: 256,
  // optional: encoding: 'terrarium' | 'mapbox' (match your DEM)
});
```

## 3. Turn the terrain on

```js
map.setTerrain({
  source: 'terrain-dem',   // name of the source above
  exaggeration: 1.0        // tweak later if you want more “mountain‑ous” look
});
```

> **Tip:** `exaggeration` does **not** fix a flat‑looking map – the map must be pitched (you already do).

---

## 4. Add a sky (the “blank horizon” disappears)

```js
// This can be called any time after the map is ready (e.g. in `map.on('load', …)`)
map.setSky({
  // 1️⃣ Base sky colour – a clear‑day blue works well
  'sky-color': '#199EF3',

  // 2️⃣ Colour of the horizon line (where sky meets ground)
  'horizon-color': '#ffffff',

  // 3️⃣ How quickly the sky colour blends into the horizon colour
  //    0 = abrupt, 1 = very smooth
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,

  // 4️⃣ Fog (haze) colour – usually a light blue or gray
  'fog-color': '#cce6ff',

  // 5️⃣ How the fog blends with the horizon colour
  //    0 = no fog at the horizon, 1 = fog starts right at the horizon
  'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,

  // 6️⃣ How the fog fades into the ground (terrain)
  //    0 = fog stops at the ground, 1 = fog reaches the ground
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
});
```

### What each property does

| Property | Effect | Typical range |
|----------|--------|---------------|
| `sky-color` | Overall colour of the sky dome | any CSS colour |
| `horizon-color` | Colour of the line where sky meets terrain | usually a lighter version of `sky-color` |
| `sky-horizon-blend` | Smoothness of the sky‑to‑horizon transition | `0 – 1` (0.4‑0.6 is common) |
| `fog-color` | Colour of the atmospheric haze that appears near the horizon | light blue, gray, or even a warm pink |
| `horizon-fog-blend` | How far the fog extends upward from the horizon | `0 – 1` (0.3‑0.6 works well) |
| `fog-ground-blend` | How far the fog reaches down onto the terrain | `0 – 1` (0.4‑0.7 gives a nice “ground‑meet‑sky” glow) |

You can experiment with the numbers until the sky looks the way you want. The defaults are fairly subtle; increasing the blend values makes the sky and fog smoother and more pronounced.

---

## 5. (Optional) Set a background colour that will be visible **under** the sky

If you ever switch the camera to `pitch: 0` (looking straight down) the sky disappears and the map’s background colour shows. It’s a good idea to set a neutral background that matches the sky’s lower tone:

```js
map.setStyle({
  // …your existing style definition…
  "background": {
    "type": "background",
    "paint": {
      "background-color": "#cce6ff"   // same as fog‑color, or a close match
    }
  }
});
```

You can also add the background layer directly in your style JSON if you prefer.

---

## 6. Full minimal example (copy‑paste ready)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Terrain + Sky + Fog</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.6.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.6.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // ---- DEM source (terrain) ----
        'terrain-dem': {
          type: 'raster-dem',
          url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json',
          tileSize: 256,
          // encoding: 'terrarium'   // uncomment if your DEM uses Terrarium encoding
        },
        // ---- Base raster imagery (optional) ----
        'sat': {
          type: 'raster',
          url: 'https://tiles.example.com/satellite/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
          tileSize: 256
        }
      },
      layers: [
        // Base imagery
        { id: 'satellite', type: 'raster', source: 'sat' },

        // Optional: a hillshade layer for extra relief
        {
          id: 'hillshade',
          type: 'hillshade',
          source: 'terrain-dem',
          paint: {
            'hillshade-method': 'multidirectional',
            'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.4,
            'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'map',
            'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(30,40,80,0.45)',
            'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,240,200,0.4)'
          }
        },

        // Background (matches fog colour)
        {
          id: 'bg',
          type: 'background',
          paint: { 'background-color': '#cce6ff' }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 70,          // you already have this
    bearing: 0,
    antialias: true    // required for terrain & sky
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ Enable terrain
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

    // 2️⃣ Add the sky + fog
    map.setSky({
      'sky-color': '#199EF3',
      'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
      'fog-color': '#cce6ff',
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the file in a browser, tilt the map (or use the mouse wheel while holding **Shift**), and you’ll see:

* A blue dome above the terrain.
* A soft white‑to‑blue horizon.
* A light haze that gently fades into the ground, giving the classic “mountain‑meets‑sky” look.

---

## 7. Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Why it happens | Fix |
|---------|----------------|-----|
| **Sky never appears** (still blank background) | `map.setTerrain()` not called **or** terrain source not loaded yet. | Call `setTerrain` *after* the DEM source is added, preferably inside `map.on('load')`. |
| **Fog is invisible** | `fog-color` is the same as `sky-color` or `horizon-fog-blend` is `0`. | Use a slightly different colour (e.g., a lighter blue) and set `horizon-fog-blend` ≥ 0.3. |
| **Sky looks too “flat”** | `sky-horizon-blend` is too low (hard edge) or `sky-color` is too saturated. | Raise `sky-horizon-blend` to 0.5‑0.7 and/or tone down `sky-color`. |
| **Sky flickers when panning** | `antialias: true` missing on map creation (required for terrain & sky). | Add `antialias: true` to the `Map` constructor. |
| **Background colour shows through the sky** | You added a `background` layer *above* the sky (higher `z-index`). | Keep the background layer **below** any terrain‑related layers (or just rely on the sky’s own colour). |

---

## 8. Going further – customizing the look

* **Sun & atmosphere** – MapLibre does **not** support `sky-type: "atmosphere"` or sun‑related properties (those belong to Mapbox GL JS). The sky is a simple colour gradient + fog.
* **Dynamic sky colour** – If you want the sky colour to change with time of day, you can call `map.setSky()` again whenever your UI updates (e.g., on a timer or based on user input).
* **Night mode** – Use a dark `sky-color` (`#0b1d2e`) and a deeper `fog-color` (`#001133`) and lower the `sky-horizon-blend` for a more dramatic effect.

---

### TL;DR

1. **Enable terrain** (`map.setTerrain({source:'your-dem'})`).  
2. **Add a sky** with `map.setSky({ … })` – set `sky-color`, `horizon-color`, `sky-horizon-blend`, `fog-color`, `horizon-fog-blend`, `fog-ground-blend`.  
3. Make sure the map is **pitched** (you already have `pitch: 70`) and **antialias** is true.  
4. Optionally add a background layer that matches the fog colour.

That’s all you need to replace the blank page background with a proper sky and a gentle haze that meets the terrain. Happy mapping!

Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste ready** recipe for putting a pre‑rendered shaded‑relief JPEG tile set under all of your vector data (including the label layer that you already have).  
Everything is done with the **standard `raster` source / `raster` layer** types – no hillshade, no DEM, no special tricks.  

---

## 1.  Add the raster source

```js
// 1️⃣  Create a raster source that points at your JPEG tiles.
//     Replace the URL template with the one that serves your tiles.
map.addSource('shaded-relief-basemap', {
  type: 'raster',
  // Example: https://mytileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg
  tiles: ['https://mytileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'],
  tileSize: 256,          // most raster tiles are 256 px; change if yours are 512
  maxzoom: 14,            // set to the highest zoom you have tiles for
  minzoom: 0,             // optional – the lowest zoom you have
  attribution: '© MyDataProvider'   // optional but recommended
});
```

*Why `type: 'raster'`?*  
A JPEG tile set is just an image – MapLibre treats it as a raster source.  
`raster-dem` is **only** for elevation data; it would try to interpret the JPEG as packed height values, which would give you a completely wrong picture.

---

## 2.  Add a raster layer that draws the source

```js
// 2️⃣  Add a raster layer that uses the source we just created.
map.addLayer({
  id: 'shaded-relief-basemap',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'shaded-relief-basemap',
  // Optional: make the imagery a little transparent if you want to see
  // underlying background colour or a faint hillshade underneath.
  paint: {
    'raster-opacity': 1.0,          // 0‑1, change to 0.9, 0.8 … if you like
    'raster-fade-duration': 0       // no fade‑in/out when tiles load (snappier)
  }
});
```

### Where does the layer go?

MapLibre draws layers **in the order they appear in the style** – first layer = bottom, last layer = top.  
If you add the raster layer *after* you have already added your vector layers, it will appear **on top** (which you don’t want).  

Two ways to guarantee the correct order:

#### a) Add it **before** you add any other layers

```js
// Add the raster layer first …
map.addLayer({ … });

// … then add your vector layers (roads, parks, labels, etc.)
map.addLayer({ id: 'roads', type: 'line', source: 'my-vector', … });
map.addLayer({ id: 'labels', type: 'symbol', source: 'my-vector', … });
```

#### b) Insert it at a specific position with `addLayer(..., beforeId)`

If you already have a style loaded and you just want to inject the basemap under everything else, do:

```js
// Find the id of the *first* layer that you want to stay on top of.
// Usually that is the first symbol (label) layer, but you can pick any.
const firstLabelLayerId = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol').id;

// Insert the raster layer right before that label layer.
map.addLayer({
  id: 'shaded-relief-basemap',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'shaded-relief-basemap',
  paint: { 'raster-opacity': 1.0 }
}, firstLabelLayerId);
```

Now the raster sits **under** all symbol layers (labels) and under any other vector layers you add later.

---

## 3.  (Optional) Tweak the raster appearance

| Property | What it does | Typical values |
|----------|--------------|----------------|
| `raster-opacity` | Transparency of the raster tiles | `0.8`‑`1.0` |
| `raster-contrast` | Boost/soften contrast (WebGL shader) | `0.5`‑`2.0` |
| `raster-saturation` | Desaturate or oversaturate the imagery | `0`‑`2` |
| `raster-fade-duration` | Fade‑in time when a new tile arrives (ms) | `0` for instant, `300` for smooth |

Example:

```js
paint: {
  'raster-opacity': 0.95,
  'raster-contrast': 1.2,
  'raster-saturation': 0.9,
  'raster-fade-duration': 0
}
```

---

## 4.  Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Shaded‑relief basemap under vector data</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // 1️⃣  Raster source (your JPEG tiles)
        'shaded-relief-basemap': {
          type: 'raster',
          tiles: ['https://mytileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'],
          tileSize: 256,
          maxzoom: 14,
          attribution: '© MyDataProvider'
        },
        // Example vector source (replace with yours)
        'my-vector': {
          type: 'vector',
          url: 'https://mytileserver.com/vector/style.json'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        // 2️⃣  Raster layer – placed first so it’s at the bottom
        {
          id: 'shaded-relief-basemap',
          type: 'raster',
          source: 'shaded-relief-basemap',
          paint: { 'raster-opacity': 1.0 }
        },
        // 3️⃣  Vector layers (roads, parks, etc.)
        {
          id: 'roads',
          type: 'line',
          source: 'my-vector',
          'source-layer': 'roads',
          paint: { 'line-color': '#ffffff', 'line-width': 2 }
        },
        // 4️⃣  Labels – they automatically sit on top
        {
          id: 'place-labels',
          type: 'symbol',
          source: 'my-vector',
          'source-layer': 'places',
          layout: {
            'text-field': ['get', 'name'],
            'text-size': 12,
            'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular', 'Arial Unicode MS Regular']
          },
          paint: { 'text-color': '#000000' }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2,
    pitch: 0,
    bearing: 0
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*What this does*  

1. Declares a **raster source** that points at your JPEG tiles.  
2. Adds a **raster layer** that draws those tiles. Because it’s the **first layer** in the style, everything else (vector layers, symbols, labels) is rendered on top of it.  
3. Shows a tiny vector layer (`roads`) and a symbol layer (`place-labels`) to illustrate that the labels stay on top.

---

## 5.  Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Why it happens | Fix |
|---------|----------------|-----|
| The JPEG tiles appear **gray or black** | The source was added as `type: 'raster-dem'` (DEM expects packed elevation) | Use `type: 'raster'`. |
| The basemap covers the labels | The raster layer was added **after** the label layer, or you didn’t specify a `beforeId`. | Insert the raster layer **before** any label/symbol layers, or use `addLayer(..., beforeId)`. |
| Tiles load very slowly / flicker | Default `raster-fade-duration` (300 ms) adds a fade‑in for each tile. | Set `'raster-fade-duration': 0` in the `paint` object. |
| You see a white/blank background when you zoom out beyond the raster’s maxzoom | No tiles exist at that zoom level. | Either provide lower‑zoom tiles, or set `maxzoom` on the source to the highest zoom you have and let the map fall back to the background colour for higher levels. |
| You want the basemap to be **partially transparent** so underlying background colour shows | You forgot to set `raster-opacity`. | Add `'raster-opacity': 0.8` (or any 0‑1 value) in the `paint` object. |

---

## 6.  TL;DR – the 3‑step checklist

1. **Create a raster source** (`type: 'raster'`) that points at your JPEG tile URL template.  
2. **Add a raster layer** that uses that source.  
3. **Insert the layer before any vector/symbol layers** (either by adding it first or by using the `beforeId` argument).  

That’s it – your pre‑rendered shaded‑relief basemap will now sit snugly under all of your vector data, with labels still on top. 🎉

If you later decide you want *real* hillshade or 3‑D terrain, you’ll need a **DEM** (`raster-dem`) and the corresponding `hillshade`, `color‑relief`, or `setTerrain()` calls – but for a static JPEG basemap the steps above are all you need.
